<div class="grid" style="margin-top:14px">
<div class="card"><h3>Siting</h3><ul>
<li>On the grass immediately <b>east of the covered deck</b>, next to the hot tub’s east bay and close to the deck edge. A short path and a couple of steps off the east corner of the deck put the door a few steps from the tub. The deck itself stays as it is — sofa, French doors and porch roof are unchanged.</li>
<li>The permit first-floor plan (sheet A4) shows the deck as <b>31′-0″ wide by about 11 ft deep</b>, on 6×6 posts with 5¼×11⅞ PSL beams and 5/4 wood decking. The included hot tub already sits in the east bay, behind the later cedar privacy screen.</li>
<li>Foundation is three 6×6 pressure-treated skids on a compacted gravel pad (a short pier only if the grade needs it). This lawn is relatively flat.</li>
<li>Whitewater Township RC-1 setbacks are 15 ft side and 30 ft rear; Woodland Trails allows accessory buildings 75 ft back from the road right-of-way. This backyard corner is well behind the road setback; confirm the east side-yard to the property line before staking.</li>
<li>The county septic sketch puts a reserve area in the backyard, roughly 15–30 ft off the house — this is approximate, ±10–15 ft. The grass strip east of the deck is only about 11–12 ft deep, so a building set far enough from the house for chimney clearance can overlap the sketched reserve. <b>Have a pumper mark the tank, field and reserve first, then stake.</b></li>
</ul></div>
<div class="card"><h3>The pick: an 8×12 wood-fired skid sauna</h3><ul>
<li>A standalone, 4-person wood-fired skid sauna, scaled from a 9×16 framing set so it can sit right off this deck without a 16-ft projection into the yard.</li>
<li><b>8×12</b> (12 ft along the house, 8 ft deep), about 9–10 ft tall at the high side of a low (about 2.5:12) shingle shed roof. Board-and-batten siding, 2×6 walls with mineral-wool insulation. A small changing room at the west/door end, facing the deck; the hot room to the east, with benches for about four people.</li>
<li>Wood-fired stove, <b>NIPPA WB-21 or equivalent</b>, fired from outside through the far (east) wall, with a Class-A chimney on that far end, away from the house and the porch roof. The stove is about $2,299, or $2,450 with the firing extension (needed because it is fired from outside), plus a chimney kit, a non-combustible hearth pad, heat shields and a spark arrestor — stove and chimney about $3,000–$4,500.</li>
<li>DIY from the framing set, scaled to 8×12: about <b>$9,000–$16,000</b> in materials, plus the stove and chimney. Builder-built: about <b>$22,000–$40,000</b>.</li>
</ul></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Chimney, hearth, clearances, power, and permits</h3><ul>
<li><b>Class-A chimney</b> on the far (east) end, with a <b>spark arrestor</b> on the cap. Typical chimney rule: the top is at least <b>2 ft above anything within 10 ft</b> (often about 10 ft of pipe, and about 3 ft above the sauna ridge if nothing is closer). Keep the chimney about <b>10 ft from the house and the porch eaves</b>, and well clear of the deck roof and the hot tub. The east grass strip is tight for that 10 ft house clearance. If 10 ft from the house can’t be had without hitting the reserve, get a code opinion and keep the stove and chimney on the far end, clear of the eaves.</li>
<li><b>Non-combustible hearth pad</b> under the stove and at the outside firing door, sized to the stove maker’s instructions (NIPPA WB-21 or equivalent), plus the listed heat shields and wall/ceiling clearances. Don’t sit the stove on the wood skids alone.</li>
<li>Keep a clear working strip around the firing door, and don’t store firewood against the sauna wall that has the stove.</li>
<li>No 240-volt circuit is required. A short 120V/20A run for a light and an outlet is optional; it can come from the nearby deck or house circuits.</li>
<li>An accessory-building permit, and likely a wood-stove / chimney permit, should be confirmed with the county. Tell the insurer.</li>
</ul></div></div></section>

<section id="outdoors"><h2>Trails from the back door</h2><p>The west lot line borders state forest. An old two-track from the backyard reaches the Vasa Singletrack in about half a mile, and the Sand Lakes Quiet Area, Muncie Lakes and the main Vasa trailhead are each a short drive.</p>
